/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A reaction such as N2 + 3H2 → 2NH3 gives the ratio in which molecules combine.
   - The number in front of each species is its stoichiometric coefficient νi.
   - Aᵢ is the formula of species i and νᵢ its stoichiometric coefficient; the sum runs over every species in the mixture.
   - The sign convention turns the reaction into one linear equation.
   - Scaling the reaction multiplies every νᵢ.
   - One number tracks how far the reaction has gone.
   - nᵢ is the moles of species i at extent ξ (mol), nᵢ₀ is its initial moles (mol), νᵢ is its stoichiometric coefficient, and ξ is the extent (mol).
   - At the start ξ = 0. ξ is positive when the reaction proceeds as written and negative when it runs in reverse.
   - Adding the species balances gives the total moles:
   - nT is the total moles at extent ξ (mol), nT₀ is the initial total moles (mol), and Σᵢνᵢ is the sum of all stoichiometric coefficients.
*/
