/* book: lead: At fixed temperature and pressure, a closed reacting mixture moves to the composition with the lowest total Gibbs energy. This is the chemical equilibrium criterion: no further change lowers G. The same idea decides whether a reaction goes forward, backward, or stands still, and it works for one reaction or several. Process engineers use it to check feasibility before building a reactor, to set op */
/* book Figure 7.2.1: The worked isomerisation A ⇌ B at 500 K: total Gibbs energy against extent. The slope is −8567 J/mol at ξ = 0.25, so reaction runs forward, and +5134 J/mol at ξ = 0.90, so it runs back; both meet at the minimum, ξ = 0.724 mol and 5345 J below pure A, where ΔGr = 0. */
